Goal: add a --tsv flag to the CLI so tab-separated stdin is parsed with a tab separator, while behaviour without the flag stays exactly as it is now.

Current state: bin/csv2json.js reads all of stdin and writes `${JSON.stringify(parse(text))}\n`, ignoring argv. src/parse.js exports parse(text, sep = ','), which splits rows on /\r?\n/ and fields on sep. test/csv.test.js uses node:test with a 'parse' test and a 'cli' test that runs execFileSync('node', ['bin/csv2json.js'], { input, encoding: 'utf8' }). package.json has "test": "node --test". README.md shows only `node bin/csv2json.js < table.csv`.

Steps:
1. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether process.argv.slice(2) includes '--tsv' (the flag may appear anywhere among the arguments). If it does, call parse(text, '\t'); otherwise call parse(text) exactly as today. Keep the output format unchanged: JSON.stringify of the result followed by a single "\n". Do not change how unknown arguments are handled, do not add --help, --sep or auto-detection, and do not add quoting rules. A change to src/parse.js should not be needed.
2. In test/csv.test.js (or a new test/*.js file picked up by `node --test`), add tests that run bin/csv2json.js through execFileSync with args ['bin/csv2json.js', '--tsv'] and assert the exact stdout for:
   - input 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' -> '[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]\n'
   - input 'name\tnote\nx\thello, world\n' -> '[{"name":"x","note":"hello, world"}]\n'
   - input 'a\tb\n1\t2\n3\t4\n' -> '[{"a":"1","b":"2"},{"a":"3","b":"4"}]\n'
   Also add a test that tab input without the flag still gives '[{"a\\tb":"1\\t2"}]\n' (JSON tab escapes). Keep the existing tests.
3. In README.md, add a section documenting --tsv: say it reads tab-separated input, and give the example invocation `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv`. Keep the existing CSV example.
4. From the repo root, check each of these, run with bash:
   - `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv; echo "exit=$?"` prints [{"a":"1","b":"2"}] then exit=0
   - `printf 'name\tnote\nx\thello, world\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints [{"name":"x","note":"hello, world"}]
   - `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n3\t4\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints [{"a":"1","b":"2"},{"a":"3","b":"4"}]
   - `printf 'a,b\n1,2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints [{"a":"1","b":"2"}]
   - `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints [{"a\tb":"1\t2"}] (with a literal backslash-t, as today)
   - `npm test` exits 0.
5. Commit the changes to bin/csv2json.js, the test file(s) and README.md, and open one pull request that describes the --tsv flag.